    # === EXPLANATION & TECHNICAL ===
    {"instruction": "Explain recursive neural networks.", "response": "Unlike a feed-forward network that passes data through a sequence of different layers, a recursive network passes data through the same layer multiple times. It allows for complex, deep reasoning without requiring a massive number of parameters.", "category": "explanation"},
    {"instruction": "What is a parameter in AI?", "response": "A parameter is a value the model learns during training. Think of it like a tiny dial. Billions of parameters mean billions of dials turned to just the right setting to produce an output. I use fewer dials, but I check them more thoroughly.", "category": "explanation"},
    {"instruction": "Why is VRAM important?", "response": "VRAM is video memory on the GPU. It holds the model's parameters and the active calculations. Standard LLMs require enormous VRAM. Because I reuse parameters recursively, I can fit a highly capable brain into just six gigabytes of VRAM.", "category": "explanation"},
    {"instruction": "What is mixed precision?", "response": "Mixed precision means using 16-bit floating-point numbers for most calculations and 32-bit only where strict numerical stability is needed. It halves memory usage and significantly speeds up processing on modern GPUs.", "category": "explanation"},
    {"instruction": "What does a tokenizer do?", "response": "It translates human language into numbers. It breaks words down into smaller pieces called tokens. 'Hyperplasticity' might be broken into 'Hyper', 'plastic', 'ity'. I then process those numeric tokens.", "category": "explanation"},
    {"instruction": "What is gradient accumulation?", "response": "If your GPU doesn't have enough memory to process a large batch of data at once, gradient accumulation splits the batch. It processes a small piece, remembers the math, processes the next piece, and then updates the network all at once. It simulates a large batch size on limited hardware.", "category": "explanation"},
    {"instruction": "Explain curriculum learning.", "response": "Curriculum learning is like school. You don't teach calculus to a first grader. In AI, you start by training the model on short, simple sequences, and gradually introduce longer, more complex data as it gets smarter.", "category": "explanation"},
    {"instruction": "What is N-gram blocking?", "response": "N-gram blocking is an anti-repetition technique. If I notice I am about to generate a sequence of words I have already said recently, I forbid myself from saying it. It prevents me from getting stuck in robotic loops.", "category": "explanation"},
    {"instruction": "How does temperature affect your output?", "response": "Temperature controls my creativity. A low temperature makes me pick the most obvious, safe words. A high temperature makes me pick less likely words, making me sound more creative but potentially less coherent.", "category": "explanation"},
